[Music]  covid19 cases are surging in myanmar  after six months of military rule only  40 percent of its healthcare facilities  are open  [Music]  doctors and nurses led a civil  disobedience movement against the  military takeover  drawing the ire of the junta  now many medical staff are afraid to  turn up to work  further straining myanmar's beleaguered  hospitals  test and trace systems are faltering due  to public mistrust in the new  establishment  faith in medical care is waning  further boosting kovid's spread  faced with such difficulty myanmar  citizens are finding their own ways to  care for each other  this is your kovid 19 special here on dw  i'm janelle demala  and as people in myanmar find themselves  unable to rely on public services to  help them deal with a surge other  sectors of society are stepping in to  fill the void including places of  worship  it's just after dawn in yangon myanmar's  largest city  in front of this mosque the line for  oxygen refills slowly shuffles forward  people have already been waiting for  hours  desperate for medical oxygen  for loved ones with covid19  if we want to get oxygen we have to come  here early even though i came here at  around four in the morning i had to  stand back in the line as some people  had already arrived  for many in myanmar treating covid19 is  a do-it-yourself project  oxygen and hospital beds are scarce  the mosque decided to offer oxygen as a  way to help  initially we thought of distributing  meals to covid quarantine centers like  we did last year  but we changed our decision as we saw  people in desperate need of medical  oxygen  it's available here free of charge  religion ethnic background doesn't  matter  all who wait are served  it's a lifeline as most people here  can't afford to pay  prices have more than tripled with the  infection surge  the military government has not been  able to effectively manage the health  crisis  authorities are now even forcing oxygen  factories to close  so we're struggling to get enough  supplies to refill the oxygen tanks  at public hospitals there aren't enough  medical facilities and they're finding  it hard to cope with the demand for  oxygen and other essential drugs and  equipment  with the country in crisis these muslim  volunteers say they want to join with  the buddhist majority in the fight  against covet 19.  we're worried because we're muslim and a  minority here  frankly speaking we're concerned about  the potential threats against us  we just want to fill in the gap and help  people  and many in need are taking up the offer  as they have nowhere else to turn  a mintant is a journalist following the  situation in myanmar they join us from  bangkok welcome  we just saw in the report there that  dealing with kovid 19 in myanmar is  pretty much a do-it-yourself project  is that accurate  yes many people are self-treating  covet-19 at home in the last few weeks  we've seen people taking to social media  to try to either beg or look for oxygen  and other medical supplies  we've seen huge lines on oxygen plants  as well as unfortunately crematoriums  and part of the thing for this is  because people are not being admitted  into hospitals which are  functioning at very low capacity right  now because of the civil disobedience  movements  and just people's distrust of anything  controlled by the hunter at the moment  and  because of the situation people are not  being admitted until they're at very low  oxygen saturation  now  as you said there the united nations  estimates that only 40 percent of  myanmar's health care facilities are  still able to function you've spoken a  bit about this already but how else does  this manifest on the ground  yeah so i think when we think about that  40 number it's also important to  remember that uh even at 100 myanmar did  not have amazing healthcare  infrastructure and many people  especially people who are um very low  income are we're not getting very good  medical care to begin with um and  because of the coup because of the civil  disobedience movement people are not  really able to seek medical help at uh  government facilities many people are  not willing to go to government  facilities and even those who do are  seeing huge lines because healthcare  workers themselves are boycotting  these facilities and so you see many  people getting turned away even for very  routine things not not related to cobits  such as just regular dialysis treatments  things that shouldn't be life or death  situations but are beginning to turn  into such  and we're seeing many health care  workers who are are now in hiding and  trying to provide care through  alternative means but are seeing  pushback and persecution by the hunter  what kind of persecution are we talking  about  um so because healthcare workers are a  huge part of the civil disobedience  movement which is the  sort of main non-violent uh struck  movement against the the coup and the  hunter  many doctors nurses other healthcare  workers have themselves imprisoned in  recent months we've seen  soldiers essentially do sting operations  where they pretend to be patients to try  and lure doctors out of hiding  and then arrest them  the head of the covert response under  the civilian government is also in jail  and we're seeing many many doctors who  are not living in their homes anymore  who are now sort of either staying with  relatives trying to find safe houses  and other means of trying to make sure  that they  are not in places where the hotel would  be able to find them  certainly a very difficult situation but  can you tell us how are vaccines  reaching the people in the midst of all  of this  so a little over 1.7 million people are  said to be vaccinated according to the  junta's health ministry  and a little over 2 million people are  or have one vaccine and this is in the  country of 54 million people and so you  know it's a very low percentage of the  actual population and  